{"name":"Silver Willow Green","hex":"#637c5b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lustrous, white, metallic element, atomic number 47, atomic weight 107.87, symbol Ag.","Coins made from silver or any similar white metal."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from silver.","Made from another white metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver"},{"term":"willow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various deciduous trees or shrubs in the genus Salix, in the willow family Salicaceae, found primarily on moist soils in cooler zones in the northern hemisphere.","The wood of these trees."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To open and cleanse (cotton, flax, wool, etc.) by means of a willow.","To form a shape or move in a way similar to the long, slender branches of a willow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/willow"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salix geyeriana","blocks":[{"text":"Salix geyeriana is a species of willow known by the common names Geyer's willow, Geyer willow and silver willow. The type specimen was collected by the botanist Karl Andreas Geyer, for whom it was named. Its conspicuous, yellow flowers begin to bloom as early as March, to as late as the end of June."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Salixgeyeriana.jpg/330px-Salixgeyeriana.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salix_geyeriana"},{"title":"Willow","blocks":[{"text":"Willows, also called sallows and osiers, of the genus Salix, comprise around 350 species of typically deciduous trees and shrubs. They are primarily found on moist soils in cold and temperate regions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Salix_alba_Morton.jpg/330px-Salix_alba_Morton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willow"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg/500px-Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg/500px-Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"At the base of the petiole, a pair of stipules form. These may fall in spring, or last for much of the summer or even for more than one year (marcescence).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalix_silesiaca_a5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Young-catkin.jpg/500px-Young-catkin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Young-catkin.jpg/500px-Young-catkin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Young male catkin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoung-catkin.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg/500px-Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg/1280px-Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg/500px-Bourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pollard willow and woodpile in the Bourgoyen-Ossemeersen, Ghent, Belgium","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABourgoyen_knotted_willow_and_woodpile.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Kopfweiden_1.jpg/500px-Kopfweiden_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Kopfweiden_1.jpg/1280px-Kopfweiden_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Kopfweiden_1.jpg/500px-Kopfweiden_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Berlin Britzer Garten pollarded willow tree in the spring of March 2018","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKopfweiden_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Weeping_Willow_by_Pond.jpg/500px-Weeping_Willow_by_Pond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Weeping_Willow_by_Pond.jpg/500px-Weeping_Willow_by_Pond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Weeping willow, an example of a hybrid between two types of willow","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWeeping_Willow_by_Pond.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/A_Modern_Cricket_Bat.jpg/500px-A_Modern_Cricket_Bat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/A_Modern_Cricket_Bat.jpg/1280px-A_Modern_Cricket_Bat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/A_Modern_Cricket_Bat.jpg/500px-A_Mo